CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a simple colored drawing of a barber pole standing upright on a white ground, annotated with two motion labels. The pole is a tall cylinder capped by an open ellipse, which gives it a three-dimensional read; its curved surface is wrapped by broad helical stripes alternating red, white, blue, and white, tilting downward from left to right and compressing toward the silhouette edges as they turn onto the far side. Above the cylinder, a nearly closed circular arrow drawn in a horizontal plane carries its arrowhead at the upper right, indicating spin about the vertical axis; the words "Real motion" sit above it. To the right, the words "Observed motion" head a short straight arrow pointing vertically downward. Putting a horizontal rotation arrow and a vertical perception arrow in one picture sets the physical direction against the perceived one. The drawing presents the barber-pole display as a case of the aperture problem: the stripes travel around the pole as it turns, yet seen only through the narrow window of the cylinder they register as moving downward.

PROJECTION TYPE: perspective
Use the scaffold's true perspective projection after building all geometry. Use this only when the source has real vanishing-point convergence or distance-based size shrinkage; then depth should visibly affect apparent size.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Tall vertical cylinder (barber pole) standing upright, open at the top
  - Elliptical rim at the top of the cylinder drawn as a black outline ring (open cap, white interior)
  - Elliptical bottom edge of the cylinder, black outline
  - Vertical black silhouette edges down the left and right of the cylinder
  - Helical stripe pattern wrapped on the curved surface: broad red band, white gap, broad blue band, white gap, repeating (~3 red + 3 blue bands visible on the front)
  - Perspective compression of the stripes toward the left and right silhouette edges as they turn onto the far side
  - Circular rotation arrow drawn in a horizontal plane above the cylinder, nearly closed, arrowhead at the upper right indicating spin about the vertical axis
  - Text label 'Real motion' above the circular arrow
  - Text label 'Observed motion' to the right of the cylinder
  - Short straight arrow below that label pointing vertically downward
  - Optional motion-tracer overlays: a dot riding a surface point around the cylinder, and a horizontal band marker riding a stripe edge downward

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Cylinder: radius 1.0, height 5.0 world units (screen aspect ≈ 1 wide : 2.5 tall), axis along +Y, centered on the origin so it spans y = -2.5 to +2.5. Open top: draw only the elliptical rim at y=+2.5 as a black torus/line ring, interior white (no cap fill shown from this slightly-above view). Bottom rim at y=-2.5 also a black ellipse. Camera slightly above eye level (elev ≈ 12°) so the top rim reads as an ellipse about 0.28 as tall as it is wide; the bottom rim shows only a shallow curve. Stripes: helical bands painted on the curved surface, pattern period along the axis such that ~6 colored bands (3 red, 3 blue) are visible on the front face; band angular width ≈ 0.22 of the period in the stripe-normal direction, separated by equal white gaps (order red, white, blue, white). Stripes rise to the RIGHT (left end lower, right end higher) at the slider angle, default 25° above horizontal on the unrolled surface — generate as texture: color = f(u*tan? ) use phase = (v/H)*cos(theta) - (u/2π)*sin(theta)... simplest: phase = (y - k*x_angle) where k = cot(angle); tile 4 stripe periods over the height at 25°. Rotation: clockwise seen from above (front surface moves to screen-RIGHT), ω ≈ 0.6 rad/s; combined with right-rising stripes this yields apparent DOWNWARD band motion at speed v_y = ω·R / tan(theta). Colors: red #EE0000 / pure #FF0000, blue #0000FF, white #FFFFFF, outlines and text black #000000 with ~4px strokes. 'Real motion' text sits top-left above the pole; the rotation arrow is a horizontal ellipse (semi-axes 1.2 x 0.35) centered on the axis at y ≈ +3.6, drawn as a nearly closed arc with a ~30° gap, its solid triangular arrowhead at the upper-right of the ellipse pointing left/counterclockwise-in-screen along the far arc. 'Observed motion' text is to the right of the cylinder at roughly mid-height (x ≈ +2.6, y ≈ +0.6), with a straight downward arrow directly beneath it from y ≈ +0.1 to y ≈ -1.0, arrowhead at the bottom; its length scales with the computed apparent vertical speed.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
